  {
   "id": "n1",
   "scene": "recap",
   "text": "Did you feel that journey? You were oxygen in the playground air. You were breathed in through the nose, down the windpipe, and into a tiny air sac. You passed into the blood, and a red blood cell carried you. The heart pumped you to a leg muscle, where you helped release energy from food. Carbon dioxide was made, and it rode the blood back to the lungs, and out!",
   "hold": 1.0
  },
  {
   "id": "textbook",
   "scene": "textbook",
   "text": "Now for the textbook link! In Primary Five, you learn about the respiratory and circulatory systems. When you breathe in, oxygen enters your lungs and passes into your blood. Your heart pumps blood through blood vessels to every part of your body. Your cells use oxygen to release energy from food, and they make carbon dioxide. The blood carries carbon dioxide back to your lungs, and you breathe it out.",
